,-5.0424259825376794e-05,-0.0007219092221930623,-1.2516897186287679e-05,-0.2216406762599945,-0.08847855031490326,-0.31325459480285645,-0.4137999713420868,-0.09120630472898483,-0.0001062098381225951,-0.0006129055400379002,-0.00011526874004630372,-0.5407648086547852,-0.9004911184310913,-0.115064337849617,-6.997340824455023e-05,-0.9018967747688293,-0.0013584204716607928,-4.887569048150908e-06,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.04098135977983475,-0.00015209948469419032,-2.861018856492592e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,6785,6291,311,198,59,26056,37018,220,16,45340,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,17,24,92,41715,37018,16,45340,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,19,20,19732,59,37018,220,17,45340,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,21,24,51185,15,7110,2533,37314,9960,510,27,37420,91339,397,1249,11625,279,23606,1124,11520,37018,90,16,15170,87,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,17,24,92,488,1124,37018,90,16,15170,87,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,19,20,92,481,1124,37018,90,17,15170,87,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,21,24,92,284,220,15,59,701,582,1191,553,20194,17767,88,284,856,61,17,481,220,16,15,87,481,220,21,24,59,568,1096,48968,15491,9606,279,23606,438,11017,24391,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the positive solution to\n\\[\\frac 1{x^2-10x-29}+\\frac1{x^2-10x-45}-\\frac 2{x^2-10x-69}=0.\\]\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the equation \\(\\frac{1}{x^2-10x-29} + \\frac{1}{x^2-10x-45} - \\frac{2}{x^2-10x-69} = 0\\), we start by letting \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\). This substitution simplifies the equation as follows:\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"7037266598a839f50e6eb8f3079ad6abc70c82a1e9516e4408fe33514dac2382"},"seconds":40.818493366241455,"seed":1008438626788715234,"stop":"eos","text":"Let \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\). Then, we can rewrite the denominators using \\(y\\):\n\n- The first term becomes \\(\\frac{1}{y + 40}\\)\n- The second term becomes \\(\\frac{1}{y + 24}\\)\n- The third term remains \\(\\frac{2}{y}\\)\n\nThe equation now looks like:\n\n\\[\\frac{1}{y + 40} + \\frac{1}{y + 24} - \\frac{2}{y} = 0.\\]\n\nTo combine these fractions, we need a common denominator, which is \\(y(y + 40)(y + 24)\\). Thus, we rewrite each fraction with this common denominator:\n\n\\[\\frac{y(y + 24) + y(y + 40) - 2(y + 40)(y + 24)}{y(y + 40)(y + 24)} = 0.\\]\n\nThe numerator must equal zero for the whole expression to be zero:\n\n\\[y(y + 24) + y(y + 40) - 2(y + 40)(y + 24) = 0.\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying the numerator:\n\n\\[y^2 + 24y + y^2 + 40y - 2(y^2 + 64y + 960) = 0,\\]\n\\[2y^2 + 64y - 2y^2 - 128y - 1920 = 0,\\]\n\\[-64y - 1920 = 0.\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[-64y = 1920,\\]\n\\[y = -30.\\]\n\nRecalling the substitution \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\), we substitute back:\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x - 69 = -30,\\]\n\\[x^2 - 10x - 39 = 0.\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\):\n\n\\[x = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{10^2 + 4 \\cdot 39}}{2},\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 156}}{2},\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2},\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{10 \\pm 16}{2}.\\]\n\nThis yields two solutions:\n\n\\[x = \\frac{10 + 16}{2} = 13,\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{10 - 16}{2} = -3.\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, the answer is \\(\\boxed{13}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Let \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\). Then, we can rewrite the denominators using \\(y\\):\n\n- The first term becomes \\(\\frac{1}{y + 40}\\)\n- The second term becomes \\(\\frac{1}{y + 24}\\)\n- The third term remains \\(\\frac{2}{y}\\)\n\nThe equation now looks like:\n\n\\[\\frac{1}{y + 40} + \\frac{1}{y + 24} - \\frac{2}{y} = 0.\\]\n\nTo combine these fractions, we need a common denominator, which is \\(y(y + 40)(y + 24)\\). Thus, we rewrite each fraction with this common denominator:\n\n\\[\\frac{y(y + 24) + y(y + 40) - 2(y + 40)(y + 24)}{y(y + 40)(y + 24)} = 0.\\]\n\nThe numerator must equal zero for the whole expression to be zero:\n\n\\[y(y + 24) + y(y + 40) - 2(y + 40)(y + 24) = 0.\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying the numerator:\n\n\\[y^2 + 24y + y^2 + 40y - 2(y^2 + 64y + 960) = 0,\\]\n\\[2y^2 + 64y - 2y^2 - 128y - 1920 = 0,\\]\n\\[-64y - 1920 = 0.\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[-64y = 1920,\\]\n\\[y = -30.\\]\n\nRecalling the substitution \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\), we substitute back:\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x - 69 = -30,\\]\n\\[x^2 - 10x - 39 = 0.\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\):\n\n\\[x = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{10^2 + 4 \\cdot 39}}{2},\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 156}}{2},\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2},\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{10 \\pm 16}{2}.\\]\n\nThis yields two solutions:\n\n\\[x = \\frac{10 + 16}{2} = 13,\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{10 - 16}{2} = -3.\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, the answer is \\(\\boxed{13}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:571"}
